1. Auth and systemic 4xx failures delete every fax (sections 9, 10, 11). If re-login plus the repeated call fails, section 9 classifies the result as PERMANENT, and 403 is also PERMANENT. PERMANENT means an ERROR log and a delete, and it doesn't count toward the availability breaker. So a rotated password, a locked service account or a permission change would turn every fax into a deleted ERROR within minutes, with no way to replay. The same applies to any 4xx caused by configuration rather than the record, such as a 404 or 415 on a submit endpoint. Questions: Should a failed re-login be BLOCKING (entry stays pending, no attempt charged, auth breaker opens, alert fires) instead of PERMANENT? If the repeated call fails for a non-auth reason, is it classified by that failure instead of defaulting to PERMANENT? Which 4xx codes are truly record-level (for example 413 for an oversized document) and which are systemic? Should a spike in PERMANENT results open a breaker instead of deleting entries?

2. Crash after a successful send, before commit (sections 3, 12). POST /SendJobs returns a jobId, then the app crashes, rebalances or has its transaction aborted before the store write and offset commit. The transaction rolls back, and the record replays with no store entry and ambiguousSends at 0, so the fax is sent again. The ambiguity cap never sees this, because the send looked successful. Questions: Can RightFax ExternalJobId (deferred item 2) be used as a lookup or idempotency key before every submit? If yes, should that be required for this release instead of deferred? If not, what is the fallback, for example a marker written outside Kafka before POST /SendJobs?

3. Transaction scope of a punctuator tick (sections 6, 12). Section 12 says the punctuator runs in separate Kafka transactions, and rule 8 says each result is written immediately. Written is not the same as committed. As far as I know, store writes made in a punctuation commit with the stream task's transaction after the punctuation returns, and context.commit() only requests a commit. If so, a crash late in a 60-second tick rolls back every SUBMIT to POLL_JOB transition in that tick, and each of those faxes is sent again. Questions: What is the real commit granularity? How many submits can one tick contain at the call cap? Should submits per tick be capped separately to limit the damage until item 2 is solved?

4. Thread and transaction time budget (sections 12, 15 items 1 and 7). Inline submission blocks the StreamThread for login, upload and send, and a punctuator tick can block it for 60 seconds. Questions: What are the connect and read timeouts per call? What is the worst case for one inline record (all timeouts plus one re-login) and for one tick? Show that the longest gap between commits stays under transaction.timeout.ms (5 min) and the longest gap between polls stays under max.poll.interval.ms (10 min), including when RightFax is slow but the breaker hasn't opened yet. Exceeding either aborts the transaction or rebalances the group, which causes the duplicates in items 2 and 3. Define max-calls and chunk-size, and derive max.poll.records from these numbers instead of measuring it later. 10 to 20 looks high; 5 seems safer.

5. Terminal outcomes are log-only (sections 1, 10). DELIVERED, FAILED_FINAL_STATUS, SUBMISSION_UNCERTAIN, DEADLINE_EXPIRED and permanent failures all end with a log line and a delete, and the source bytes go with the entry. Questions: Is log-only a deliberate decision, and who approved it? How does support resend a fax that expired or was uncertain? How does anything downstream learn the outcome? Would a status topic for every terminal outcome, plus a DLQ carrying the source for failures, be acceptable? Both would be topology sinks committed in the same transaction. If log-only stays, document the recovery procedure, for example replaying the source topic from the topic-partition-offset in the log line.

6. 2xx from POST /SendJobs without a jobId (sections 3, 4, 9). This is logged as a permanent failure and never retried. Not retrying is right, but RightFax may have created the job, so the fax may still go out. Questions: Should this be its own uncertain outcome with an alert rather than a failure? Can ExternalJobId resolve it to a real jobId so it can be polled?

7. Deadline expiry semantics (section 10). The four-hour deadline keeps running while the breaker is open and during polling. That causes two problems. An outage longer than the remaining window expires every pending fax at once on recovery. And a fax accepted at 3h50 gets ten minutes of polling, then ends as ERROR DEADLINE_EXPIRED even though it was probably delivered. Questions: Should expiry during polling (delivery unconfirmed) be a separate outcome from expiry before submit (never sent)? Is mass expiry after a long outage the intended business result? Confirm that no expiry path ever triggers an automatic resend.

